    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d481">

                <pb facs="0577" n="503" sameAs="frus:frus1918Russiav01/pg503"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 861.00/1658</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Moscow</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                    >Summers</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi><anchor n="2" xml:id="d481-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-04-20T12:00:00+02:31">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">April 20, 1918, noon</hi></date>. <lb/> [<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Received April 24, 1.10 a.m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>388. Macgowan [reports] agent Singer
                    Company, probably Russian, imprisoned Krasnoyarsk refusal pay illegal
                    contributions. Singer agent, Tomsk, states armed men forced him pay former
                    clerks Rs. 34,000 arbitrarily claimed. Further reports Red Guards, principally
                    composed Hungarian prisoners who are carrying out arrests, seizures and
                    requisitions [omission?] local council Tomsk levied contribution Rs. 5,000,000
                    business men. Refusing payment they were arrested [by] armed Hungarians and
                    imprisoned. Macgowan informed Webster and Hicks
                    interference local affairs but was told matter amounted nothing.<anchor n="3"
                        xml:id="d481-f2"/> I have taken up energetically with central authorities
                    matter forced contributions and arrests managers American firms. Have been
                    assured that orders have been given stop all future armed [exactions] this
                    nature.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Summers</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d481-f1" n="2" type="source" xml:id="d481-f1-content">
                <p>Sent via the Consulate at Vladivostok.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d481-f2" n="3" xml:id="d481-f2-content">
                <p>For the connection of Captain Webster and
                    Major Hicks with the investigation of the arming of war
                    prisoners, see <hi rendition="#italic">ante</hi>, <ref target="#pg483">p.
                        483</ref>, and <ref target="frus1918Russiav02">vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">ii</hi></ref>, chap. i.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
